"At the beginning of the surah, it is described what great news the deniers ask one another about. This news is Prophethood and, in particular, the Day of Resurrection. They have fallen into disagreement regarding this news; some believe, some deny, and some hesitate. Allah rejects their denial and disagreements, emphasizing that the news is certain and that they will come to know in the future.\n\nIt is stated that the earth was created as a bed and the mountains as pegs. This analogy conveys that the earth is like a bed prepared for human beings, and the mountains are like pillars holding it steady. The wisdom behind the creation of mountains includes numerous benefits, such as protecting the earth from tremors, separating the seas and landmasses, and ensuring the flow of rivers.\n\nThe creation of human beings in pairs, sleep being made a rest and cessation, the night a covering, and the day a time of livelihood are described. Sleep is described as a cessation and rest; the night covers like a garment, and the day is a time for work and livelihood.\n\nThe creation of the heaven as seven strong structures, the sun as a radiant lamp, the pouring down of abundant water from the clouds, and thereby producing grains, plants, and luxuriant gardens are described. All of these are proofs demonstrating the power and blessings of Allah.\n\nIt is announced that the Day of Decision is an appointed time, that the Horn (Sur) will be blown, and people will come in crowds. On that day, the heaven will be opened and the mountains will be set in motion, becoming a mirage. Hell has been prepared as a place of ambush for the transgressors, and they will abide therein for ages.\n\nElmalılı states that the word 'ahqab' is the plural of 'huqb', and that huqb means an epoch comprising consecutive years. There are different interpretations of this word, such as eighty-some years or seventy thousand years. However, the fact that 'ahqab' denotes a finite duration does not mean that the punishment of Hell will come to an end; because the meaning of succession can extend indefinitely. Furthermore, it is explicitly stated in other verses that the disbelievers will abide in Hell eternally.\n\nAs a punishment for the transgressors, there is boiling water and purulence. 'Hamim' is scalding water, and 'ghassaq' is the purulent discharge flowing from the bodies of the dwellers of Hell. This punishment is a fully fitting recompense for their denial in the worldly life. The verse declares that the torment is continuous and will be increased: 'So taste, we will never increase you in anything except torment.'\n\nFor the righteous, however, there is triumph, gardens, vineyards, maidens of equal age with swelling breasts, and full cups. In Paradise, neither vain talk nor falsehood is heard. These blessings are granted as a sufficient gift from their Lord. The word 'Lord' emphasizes that Allah is the Lord of the heavens, the earth, and whatever is between them; no one can address Him.\n\nOn the Day of Resurrection, the Spirit and the angels will stand in ranks. No one will speak except those permitted by the Most Merciful. Those who are permitted will speak the truth. There are different interpretations regarding who the Spirit is: Gabriel, an angel appointed over the souls, or another creation. Knowledge concerning these details is with Allah.\n\nThat day is the True Day. Whosoever wills, let him take a path toward his Lord. This path consists of speaking the truth and obeying through faith and righteousness. Allah warns people of an imminent punishment. On that day, a person will look at what his hands have sent forth, and the disbeliever will say, 'Would that I were dust!' According to some narrations, animals will be resurrected, and after their dues are settled, they will become dust; and the disbeliever will desire to be like them."